  \n- B5 pair.  \n\nStill fragmented.  \n\nMaybe discard W8? W8 is a singleton, and if I discard W8, I keep W4 which might combine with W3 from meld? Actually meld is exposed, so I can't use W3 from meld in a new chow because meld is fixed. So W4 can't combine with W3 from meld. So W4 is standalone.  \n\nDiscard W8: then I have W4, W7, W7. Could form W4W5W6 or W5W6W7 or W6W7W8, but W6 scarce, W5 not in hand. Not great.  \n\nDiscard T1: then I have T2, T4, T5. T2 is alone, T4T5 can form chow. T2 might combine with T3 or T1 (but T1 gone). So T2 becomes less useful.  \n\nDiscard T2: then T1 alone, T4T5.  \n\nDiscard T4 or T5: breaks the T4T5 sequence.  \n\nI think the best discard is one of the isolated tiles: W4 or W8 or T1. Among these, W4 is a simple tile (number 4), not terminal, and not many out? Unshown W4: 4 left, so safe.  \n\nCheck opponents' discards for safety:  \n- Next player: played W9, B7, F4, B6, T8, W2, B9, J1, W6, J1. So they discarded W2 and W6, but not W4.  \n- Previous player: played B5, W6, W5, T9, F3, T4, T1, F2, W2, F1, B7. So they discarded W5, W2, but not W4.  \n- Across: played B9, B7, T5, W7, B2, B5, W6, B2, T5, B8. No W4.  \n\nW4 seems relatively safe.  \n\nThus, I'll discard W4 to keep potential for chows with T1T2 and T4T5, and keep pairs B5 and W7.  \n\nAnswer: Play W4.  \n\nBut let's confirm the format:  \n\nSituation: I have 10 hand tiles with one exposed Chow, needing three more melds and a pair. Hand is fragmented across suits with potential pairs in B5 and W7. Many needed tiles are scarce.  \nEvaluation: Discarding a less useful singleton like W4 is safe and maintains flexibility for forming chows and pairs.  \nReason: W4 is isolated, not well-connected given current hand and exposed meld, and appears safe based on opponents' discards. Keeping T1T2 and T4T5 sequences offers more chow potential.  \nAnswer: Play W4.  \n\nSo final output:  \n\nSituation: Hand has one exposed Chow [W1,W2,W3], and hand tiles: W2, W4, W7, W7, W8, T1, T2, T4, T5, B5, B5. Need three more melds and a pair. Many needed tiles are scarce.  \nEvaluation: Best to discard isolated tile W4 to maintain chow potential with T1T2 and T4T5, and keep pairs B5 and W7.  \nReason: W4 is not well-connected, safe based on opponent discards, and discarding it keeps more useful sequences and pairs.  \nAnswer: Play W4."}
